Default (IN-PACKAGE "COMMON-LISP-USER") fails after the prelude is loaded
=========================================================================

Symptom
-------

When bocl starts normally (prelude loaded), the default end-of-startup
IN-PACKAGE to COMMON-LISP-USER fails with:

    In bocl_in_package:
    ERROR: IN-PACKAGE: no such package COMMON-LISP-USER

In --batch -q mode this is accompanied by the user expression producing
NO output, e.g.:

    $ printf '(print (+ 40 2))\n' | sources/bocl --batch -q
    In bocl_in_package:
    ERROR: IN-PACKAGE: no such package COMMON-LISP-USER
    (no "42" is printed)

With --no-prelude the error does NOT occur and evaluation works:

    $ echo '(print (+ 1 2))' | sources/bocl --no-prelude --batch
    3
    bocl>

So the failure is tied to the prelude having been loaded.

Where it happens
----------------

main() drives, in order (sources/main.c):

  1. kernel_initialize()                       (main.c:379)
       -> packages.c:325 creates COMMON-LISP-USER:
          COMMON_LISP_USER_Package = package_new("COMMON-LISP-USER", ...)
  2. load bootstrap/prelude.lisp               (main.c:386-395)
  3. default package designator = "COMMON-LISP-USER" (main.c:285)
  4. if(package) bocl_in_package(bocl_read_cstring(package))  (main.c:445-446)

bocl_in_package (sources/bocl.c:57):

    Ref bocl_in_package(Ref package_designator){
        Ref pkg = package_find(package_designator);
        if(nullp(pkg)){
            ERROR("IN-PACKAGE: no such package %s", ...); }
        set_symbol_value(S(*PACKAGE*), pkg);
        return pkg; }

The package IS created at step 1, yet package_find() returns NIL at step 4
(after the prelude). The error is therefore not "package never existed"
but "package no longer resolvable through this lookup once the prelude has
run".

Likely cause (to confirm)
-------------------------

bocl uses the hybrid C/Lisp true-package design: the prelude (re)builds the
real CL package structures in Lisp (cl-exports.lisp is auto-generated, the
prelude loads under *PACKAGE* = CL). Two plausible mechanisms, to be
verified:

  a. The prelude replaces / re-registers the package table, so the C-side
     COMMON_LISP_USER_Package created by package_new() at startup is no
     longer the object the post-prelude registry returns for the name
     "COMMON-LISP-USER" (stale binding / shadowed registry).

  b. bocl_read_cstring("COMMON-LISP-USER") returns a SYMBOL designator
     interned in the current *PACKAGE*, and package_find()'s symbol-
     designator path does not resolve it the same way after the prelude
     (e.g. it compares the package object rather than the name string).

Either way the *default* startup path emits an error on every normal run.

Impact
------

  - Every normal (prelude-loaded) startup prints a spurious-looking error.
  - In --batch -q, the user form after it appears to be dropped (no output),
    which suggests *PACKAGE* is left in a bad state (or the REPL/eval path is
    disturbed) rather than this being a cosmetic notice only. This is the
    part that makes it a likely real bug rather than a harmless message.

Suggested investigation
-----------------------

  1. Add a probe just before main.c:446 to print whether package_find()
     resolves "COMMON-LISP-USER" both as a string designator and as the
     symbol returned by bocl_read_cstring(), to distinguish causes (a)/(b).
  2. Check whether the prelude DELETE-PACKAGE / re-creates COMMON-LISP-USER
     (or USE-PACKAGE rewiring) such that the C COMMON_LISP_USER_Package
     handle goes stale; if so, look it up by name at IN-PACKAGE time instead
     of relying on the startup handle.
  3. Confirm whether *PACKAGE* is left NIL/garbage after the failed
     IN-PACKAGE, which would explain the suppressed --batch -q output.

Notes
-----

  - Reproduced on Darwin/arm64 (Apple clang) at v0.0.59. The prelude load is
    slow (~45 s), so iterate with a probe rather than many full runs.
  - Found incidentally while validating the build/packaging deliverables; not
    introduced by them (it reproduces on the unmodified startup path).
